00:08:56,490 --> 00:09:00,220
一般来说，关于记忆的研究聚焦于过去，
Traditionally, memory research has really focused on the past,

178
00:09:00,330 --> 00:09:03,520
就在前几年，比如我自己的研究者们
and in the last few years, researchers such as myself

179
00:09:03,630 --> 00:09:06,990
开始探究我们想象未来的能力
have been looking at the ability to imagine the future

180
00:09:07,100 --> 00:09:10,230
以及记忆如何在其中发挥作用。
and how memory might actually play a role in that.

181
00:09:10,340 --> 00:09:13,400
Donna和她的团队设计了一个实验
Donna and her team set up an experiment

182
00:09:13,510 --> 00:09:17,740
来确定海马体和我们的记忆到底如何
to determine just how the hippocampus and our memories

183
00:09:17,850 --> 00:09:20,570
帮助我们认识到未来的我们。
help us perceive our future selves.

184
00:09:20,680 --> 00:09:21,810
我们要做的是
So, what we do is

185
00:09:21,920 --> 00:09:23,610
我们让实验对象来实验室
we have subjects come into the lab

186
00:09:23,720 --> 00:09:27,380
我们让他们回忆大约100个记忆。
and we have them retrieve around 100 memories.

187
00:09:27,490 --> 00:09:31,480
我记得我要去德国看望我的堂（表）亲。
I remember going to visit my cousin in Germany.

188
00:09:31,600 --> 00:09:32,780
我们随着沙滩走着，
We walked along the beach,

189
00:09:32,900 --> 00:09:34,550
在终点，有一个洞。
and at the end, there was a cave.

190
00:09:34,670 --> 00:09:37,860
我给我奶奶一个贝壳作为圣诞礼物。
And I gave my grandmother a seashell for Christmas.

191
00:09:37,970 --> 00:09:39,360
对于每一个记忆，
And for each memory,

192
00:09:39,470 --> 00:09:42,230
它们塑造了一个人，一个地点，一个物体
they identify a person, a place, and an object

193
00:09:42,340 --> 00:09:43,730
这些似乎都很重要。
that might be important.

194
00:09:43,840 --> 00:09:45,230
-高速公路。 -我母亲。
-The Autobahn. -My mother.

195
00:09:45,340 --> 00:09:46,430
-两个企鹅。 -波士顿。
-Two penguins. -Boston.

196
00:09:46,540 --> 00:09:48,030
-我的书包。 -奥克兰滨水区。
-My book bag. -Auckland waterfront.

197
00:09:48,150 --> 00:09:49,530
-我的女朋友，Sarah。 -医院。
-My girlfriend, Sarah. -The hospital.

198
00:09:49,650 --> 00:09:51,240
-我的搭档，Wayne。 -东部海滩。
-My partner, Wayne. -Eastern Beach.

199
00:09:51,350 --> 00:09:52,870
-阿姆斯特丹机场。 -洞穴。
-Amsterdam airport. -A cave.

200
00:09:52,980 --> 00:09:56,910
一周后，Donna带着她的研究对象回来了，
A week later, Donna brings her subjects back,

201
00:09:57,020 --> 00:09:59,380
让他们配置上功能磁共振成像（大脑成像技术）机器，
places them in an fMRI machine,

202
00:09:59,490 --> 00:10:03,410
并向他们展示他们记忆中的一些细节。
and shows them details from their recalled memories.

203
00:10:03,530 --> 00:10:06,250
但是她故意混乱一些细节。
But she deliberately jumbles the details.

204
00:10:06,360 --> 00:10:08,090
一个实验对象的记忆
An object from one memory

205
00:10:08,200 --> 00:10:11,620
混入了关于其他的地点的记忆
has been grouped with a place from another memory

206
00:10:11,740 --> 00:10:14,060
或是关于另一个人的记忆。
and a person from yet another.

207
00:10:14,170 --> 00:10:17,160
Donna然后让他们用这些混乱记忆
Donna then asks them to make a story

208
00:10:17,270 --> 00:10:19,660
编一个故事，
out of these mixed up memories,

209
00:10:19,780 --> 00:10:22,870
并想象一些从未发生过的事情
to imagine something that has not happened yet

210
00:10:22,980 --> 00:10:26,000
但是很可能会发生的。
but potentially could.

211
00:10:26,120 --> 00:10:27,300
我们问他们，
We ask them,

212
00:10:27,420 --> 00:10:30,040
对于他们现在看到的人、地点和事物，
for each person, place, object that they're seeing now,

213
00:10:30,150 --> 00:10:33,040
来想象一个未来可能发生在他们身上的事件
to imagine a future event that might happen to them

214
00:10:33,160 --> 00:10:35,150
或许就在未来五年内。
within the next five years or so.

215
00:10:35,260 --> 00:10:39,650
当参与者在想象他们的未来时，
While the participants are imagining their futures,

216
00:10:39,760 --> 00:10:42,190
Donna测量了他们大脑活动。
Donna measures their brain responses.

217
00:10:42,300 --> 00:10:43,320
让她惊讶的是，
To her surprise,

218
00:10:43,430 --> 00:10:45,760
脑中对于储存过去记忆非常重要的部分，
the part of the brain that is vital

219
00:10:45,870 --> 00:10:49,130
海马体，
to storing memories of the past, the hippocampus,

220
00:10:49,240 --> 00:10:52,000
伴随着活动闪闪生辉。
is blazing with activity.

221
00:10:52,110 --> 00:10:55,230
海马体的确发挥着非常重要的作用，
The hippocampus is playing a really important role,

222
00:10:55,350 --> 00:10:56,800
不仅在于记忆，
not only in remembering,

223
00:10:56,910 --> 00:11:00,270
同样也使我们构建出对未来的模拟。
but also allowing us to build these future simulations.

224
00:11:00,380 --> 00:11:03,670
记忆不仅对过去非常重要，
Memory is important not only for the past,

225
00:11:03,790 --> 00:11:05,610
对未来也很重要，
but also for the future,

226
00:11:05,720 --> 00:11:08,290
这样就可以建立一种自我存在感。
for building up that sense of who we are.

227
00:11:10,430 --> 00:11:15,150
我们的记忆对于形成我们的身份非常重要，
Our memories are crucial to forming our identities,

228
00:11:15,270 --> 00:11:17,020
但是有一个科学家小组发现
but one group of scientists have discovered

229
00:11:17,130 --> 00:11:19,320
我们的记忆可以被操纵
that our memories can be manipulated

230
00:11:19,440 --> 00:11:21,690
而我们无法发现。
without our even knowing it.

231
00:11:21,810 --> 00:11:25,040
那么我们就是我们认为的我们么？
Are we really who we think we are?

232
00:11:27,170 --> 00:11:31,120
我们记忆的能力非常卓越。
Our ability to remember is truly remarkable.

233
00:11:31,240 --> 00:11:33,430
在我们的一生中，
In the course of our lives,

234
00:11:33,540 --> 00:11:38,300
平均每人可认知100,000个单词，
the average person will grasp the meaning of 100,000 words,

235
00:11:38,410 --> 00:11:41,030
认识大约1,700个人，
get to know around 1,700 people,

236
00:11:41,150 --> 00:11:44,540
读超过1,000本书。
and read over 1,000 books.

237
00:11:44,650 --> 00:11:48,370
根据这些大量的经验储存，
From these vast mental stores of experience

238
00:11:48,490 --> 00:11:51,080
我们互相建立起了自我身份，
we each build our own identity,

239
00:11:51,190 --> 00:11:54,950
这也是一种我们独特的记忆模式。
a pattern of memories that is uniquely ours.

240
00:11:55,060 --> 00:11:59,950
但是如果这些记忆被重写呢？
But what if those memories could be rewritten?

241
00:12:00,070 --> 00:12:04,240
我们会改变对我们自己的认知么？
Could we change who we are?

242
00:12:08,670 --> 00:12:10,360
神经系统科学家Tali Sharot
Neuroscientist Tali Sharot

243
00:12:10,480 --> 00:12:12,830
来自伦敦大学，
from the University College London

244
00:12:12,950 --> 00:12:14,170
还有Micah Edelson
and Micah Edelson

245
00:12:14,280 --> 00:12:17,570
来自以色列威茨曼研究所
from the Weizmann Institute of Science in Israel

246
00:12:17,680 --> 00:12:20,270
他们都喜欢参加晚餐聚会。
love going to dinner parties.

247
00:12:20,390 --> 00:12:22,080
但是他们并不仅去Happy，
But they're not just having fun.

248
00:12:22,190 --> 00:12:23,940
也为了科学。
They're doing it for the sake of science.

249
00:12:24,060 --> 00:12:30,060
他们研究社会压力如何改变我们。
They study how social pressures alter who we are.

250
00:12:30,200 --> 00:12:31,280
来，想象一个场景
Well, imagine a situation

251
00:12:31,400 --> 00:12:33,190
你聚会中坐着。
that you're sitting in a dinner party.

252
00:12:33,300 --> 00:12:36,590
你有一些关于发生过的一些事非常有趣的记忆，
You have a pretty good memory of some situation that happened,

253
00:12:36,700 --> 00:12:40,490
你想通过一定方法来回忆。
and you actually remember it happening in a certain way,

254
00:12:40,610 --> 00:12:42,760
但是你所有的朋友都告诉你，
but all of your friends are telling you

255
00:12:42,880 --> 00:12:44,300
你错了。
that you're actually wrong.

256
00:12:44,410 --> 00:12:45,970
他们说有些别的事发生了。
They're saying that something else happened.

257
00:12:46,080 --> 00:12:47,970
Tali拿了雨伞，
And Tali got the umbrella, and she slid the umbrella

258
00:12:48,080 --> 00:12:49,770
-从信箱呢里。 -你确定那是Tali？
- through the letterbox. - Are you sure it was Tali?

259
00:12:49,880 --> 00:12:51,910
不不不。我非常确定那不是我。
No, no, no. I'm quite sure it wasn't me.

260
00:12:52,020 --> 00:12:53,970
不对，那的确是你。你真的拿着那把伞。
No, it was definitely you. You were definitely holding the umbrella.

261
00:12:54,090 --> 00:12:55,580
那是Steve。
It was definitely Steve.

262
00:12:55,690 --> 00:12:57,280
尽管我真的认为是Tali。
I really thought it was Tali, though.

263
00:12:57,390 --> 00:12:58,950
不，因为我们对Steve
No, because we were really impressed

264
00:12:59,060 --> 00:13:00,610
要把伞钩在那里印象非常深刻。
that Steve managed to hook it around.

265
00:13:00,730 --> 00:13:02,180
想起来了？我们还在那里造势，
Remember? We had that big fanfare

266
00:13:02,300 --> 00:13:03,680
因为我们都在雨中。
because we were out in the rain.

267
00:13:03,800 --> 00:13:05,720
当我们根据别人意见
When someone changes their memory

268
00:13:05,830 --> 00:13:08,820
改变记忆时，
so that it fits other people's opinion,

269
00:13:08,940 --> 00:13:11,690
我们真的改变了脑中代表记忆
do we actually change a signal in the brain

270
00:13:11,800 --> 00:13:14,560
的信号，
that is representative of the memory,

271
00:13:14,670 --> 00:13:17,360
还是我们只是在迎合他人？
or is it just that we try to please other people?

272
00:13:17,480 --> 00:13:19,830
社会压力
Social pressures

273
00:13:19,950 --> 00:13:21,800
可以使我们的故事发生变化。
can make us change the way we tell stories.

274
00:13:21,910 --> 00:13:26,710
但是这是否会让我们自己改变对事情的看法？
But can they also make us change the stories we tell ourselves?

275
00:13:26,820 --> 00:13:29,180
或是我们确确实实的记忆？
Our actual memories?

276
00:13:29,290 --> 00:13:33,010
Talihe Micah设计了一个实验，
Tali and Micah set up an experiment to find out,

277
00:13:33,130 --> 00:13:36,180
这次不在餐馆，而在实验室。
not in a restaurant, but in a lab.

278
00:13:36,300 --> 00:13:38,720
他们带来了一组志愿者，
They bring in a group of volunteers

279
00:13:38,830 --> 00:13:40,450
一起看一部电影。
to watch a film together.

280
00:13:40,570 --> 00:13:42,020
之后，
Afterwards,

281
00:13:42,130 --> 00:13:44,960
志愿者需要回答关于这部电影的一些基本问题
the volunteers answer basic questions about the film

282
00:13:45,070 --> 00:13:47,240
这样来检测他们记忆。
to test what they remember.

283
00:13:49,540 --> 00:13:50,800
几天后，
A few days later,

284
00:13:50,910 --> 00:13:53,600
参与者回答了同样的问题
the participants take the same questionnaire

285
00:13:53,710 --> 00:13:55,100
但这次有一个脑部扫描仪实施检测，
inside a brain scanner,

286
00:13:55,210 --> 00:14:00,740
这次，Micah和Tali使用社会压力。
only this time, Micah and Tali apply a social pressure.

287
00:14:00,850 --> 00:14:03,740
他们仍面对一些假答案
This time, they were exposed to fake answers

288
00:14:03,860 --> 00:14:07,510
这些假答案通过小组的其他成员大概的给出
that were supposedly given by their fellow group members.

289
00:14:07,630 --> 00:14:09,880
小组开始相信
The group is led to believe

290
00:14:10,000 --> 00:14:12,120
其参与测试的人
that the others who took the test

291
00:14:12,230 --> 00:14:16,260
记得电影中的角色并不戴帽子。
remembered the character in the film was not wearing a hat.

292
00:14:16,370 --> 00:14:20,060
并且大多数人都改变了他们的答案，
And most people changed their answers

293
00:14:20,170 --> 00:14:22,030
以与大家一致。
to go along with the crowd.

294
00:14:22,140 --> 00:14:23,560
大约70%的案例，
Almost 70% of the cases,

295
00:14:23,680 --> 00:14:26,970
参与者得到了一致的错误答案，
the participants conformed and they gave a wrong answer,

296
00:14:27,080 --> 00:14:28,570
尽管他们开始对正确的答案
even though they were initially pretty confident

297
00:14:28,680 --> 00:14:30,000
非常有信心。
about the correct answer.

298
00:14:30,120 --> 00:14:34,940
但是他们仅是表面上遵从社会准则，
But were they just outwardly complying with a social norm,

299
00:14:35,050 --> 00:14:38,560
还是他们的记忆的确改变了？
or did their memories actually change?

300
00:14:40,460 --> 00:14:42,680
接下来的几周后我们重新测试了他们，
So, we test them again a week after,

301
00:14:42,800 --> 00:14:46,350
一旦我们不再施加社会压力。
once we've removed the social pressure.

302
00:14:46,470 --> 00:14:47,890
我们认为他们仍做出了错误的答案，
And we assume that if they're still making an error,

303
00:14:48,000 --> 00:14:51,460
这说明他们的记忆的确被改变了。
that means that their memory was actually changed.

304
00:14:51,570 --> 00:14:53,060
Tali和Micah发现
Tali and Micah found

305
00:14:53,170 --> 00:14:55,860
大多数受试者深信错误的答案
that most test subjects stuck with the wrong answer

306
00:14:55,980 --> 00:14:57,800
即使没有社会压力。
even without peer pressure.

307
00:14:57,910 --> 00:15:02,400
这种错误已经根植于他们的大脑。
The falsehood has taken root in their brains.

308
00:15:02,520 --> 00:15:06,940
这引起了长期的记忆错误，
It actually causes a long-lasting memory error,

309
00:15:07,050 --> 00:15:08,940
通过使用我们脑中的数据，
and using our brain data,

310
00:15:09,060 --> 00:15:10,710
我们可以鉴定出
we are able to actually identify

311
00:15:10,820 --> 00:15:15,280
什么时候会发生这种长期的错误记忆。
when such a long-lasting memory error will occur.

312
00:15:15,390 --> 00:15:18,650
这些改变记忆的人的大脑
The brains of those people who changed their memories

313
00:15:18,760 --> 00:15:21,590
不仅在海马体中表现出了很高的活性，
showed high activity not just in the hippocampus,

314
00:15:21,700 --> 00:15:23,160
（储存记忆的地方）
where memories are stored,

315
00:15:23,270 --> 00:15:25,220
同样脑的联系情绪和社会反映的
but also in a part of the brain

316
00:15:25,340 --> 00:15:28,830
部分也活跃起来，
that is connected with emotional and social responses,

317
00:15:28,940 --> 00:15:30,310
这部分是杏仁体。
the amygdala.

318
00:15:34,950 --> 00:15:36,370
我们对于
A lot of what we know

319
00:15:36,480 --> 00:15:38,100
杏仁体及其功能了解很多
about the amygdala and its function

320
00:15:38,220 --> 00:15:40,010
不过是从动物世界中了解的。
comes from the animal world.

321
00:15:40,120 --> 00:15:43,180
那么我们对于情绪的反映——
So our reaction to anything that's emotional --

322
00:15:43,290 --> 00:15:46,380
比如我们突然听到一阵噪音而被吓到
if we suddenly hear a noise which is frightening

323
00:15:46,490 --> 00:15:49,620
或是在某人脸上加上某种情绪
or of processing emotional expressions on one's face --

324
00:15:49,730 --> 00:15:52,350
杏仁体在这些功能中都发挥重要功能。
the amygdala is crucial for all of these functions.

325
00:15:52,470 --> 00:15:56,490
而且它或许还可以帮助我们增强记忆
And it probably helps us increase memory

326
00:15:56,600 --> 00:15:59,490
因为在一种恐惧情绪的环境中，
because, in a fearful or emotional situation,

327
00:15:59,610 --> 00:16:02,030
杏仁体的活性加强
the amygdala activation is heightened

328
00:16:02,140 --> 00:16:03,660
而这种活性的增加
and it also increases activation

329
00:16:03,780 --> 00:16:05,880
也关系到像海马体的一些结构。
in related structures like the hippocampus.

330
00:16:07,350 --> 00:16:10,440
杏仁体像是夜总会的看门保镖。
The amygdala is like a bouncer at a nightclub.

331
00:16:10,550 --> 00:16:12,770
它决定了那些记忆
It decides which memories

332
00:16:12,890 --> 00:16:15,110
可以参加塑造我们生活历史的“聚会”。
get to play a part in shaping our life histories.

333
00:16:15,220 --> 00:16:18,880
只有那些带有情感分量的才允许进入。
The ones that carry emotional weight are allowed in.

334
00:16:18,990 --> 00:16:23,450
而其他的则不被允许。
The ones that don't are not.

335
00:16:23,560 --> 00:16:26,520
那些改变了记忆的受试者
The participants who changed their memories

336
00:16:26,630 --> 00:16:30,160
他们迫于达到一致的压力而受到情感上的影响，
were emotionally affected by the pressure to conform,

337
00:16:30,270 --> 00:16:32,860
于是他们的杏仁体就非常活跃，
setting their amygdalas ablaze,

338
00:16:32,970 --> 00:16:36,030
虚假的记忆就趁机溜进。
and the false memory snuck in.

339
00:16:36,140 --> 00:16:38,160
这就通过观察杏仁体的激活，
So, by looking at amygdala activation,

340
00:16:38,280 --> 00:16:39,570
我们可以确切的预测
we can actually predict

341
00:16:39,680 --> 00:16:42,440
哪些记忆会发生长期的改变
which memories are gonna be changed for a very long time

342
00:16:42,550 --> 00:16:43,800
而又有哪些不会。
and which are not.

343
00:16:43,920 --> 00:16:45,670
你要知道
You have to realize

344
00:16:45,780 --> 00:16:48,470
记忆不像录像带。
that memories are not like a videotape.

345
00:16:48,590 --> 00:16:50,610
因为人们对于
'Cause people can be extremely confident

346
00:16:50,720 --> 00:16:52,950
他们认为发生的事的记忆
that things happened like they think they happened

347
00:16:53,060 --> 00:16:54,150
具有极度信心。
when they didn't.

348
00:16:54,260 --> 00:16:57,020
我们的记忆不仅是个唱片
Our memories are not just a record

349
00:16:57,130 --> 00:16:59,890
记录了发生在我们生活中的事件
of the events that took place in our lives.

350
00:17:00,000 --> 00:17:03,660
它们是可以改变而且易错的。
They are malleable and fallible.

351
00:17:03,770 --> 00:17:08,800
我们的身份是社会中不断的信息输入造就的。
Our identities are created with constant input from our society.

352
00:17:08,910 --> 00:17:11,540
没有一个人是一座孤岛。
No man is an island.

353
00:17:13,180 --> 00:17:15,870
但我们能够更进一步
But could we go a step further

354
00:17:15,980 --> 00:17:20,640
重新塑造一个人的身份么？
and deliberately re-engineer someone's identity?

355
00:17:20,750 --> 00:17:22,080
为了这个目的，
To do that,

356
00:17:22,190 --> 00:17:25,140
你需要能够窥探一个人最深处的思维，
you have to be able to peer into a person's innermost thoughts,

357
00:17:25,260 --> 00:17:27,680
不论你是否相信，
and believe it or not,

358
00:17:27,790 --> 00:17:31,730
这项技术已经存在了。
that technology is already here.

359
00:17:36,840 --> 00:17:40,710
某一程度上说，我们全都是演员。
We are all actors, to some extent.

360
00:17:41,820 --> 00:17:44,250
我们会变成谁
Who we appear to be can change

361
00:17:44,360 --> 00:17:48,180
取决于我们的情绪或我们维持的关系。
depending on our mood or the company we keep.

362
00:17:48,300 --> 00:17:50,150
但有那么一次
But there is one time

363
00:17:50,260 --> 00:17:54,120
而真正的我们出现的时候——
when who we really are comes to the fore --

364
00:17:54,230 --> 00:17:56,620
是当我们做梦的时候。
when we dream.

365
00:17:56,740 --> 00:18:01,300
如果我们可以看到我们的梦境并对其进行研究呢？
What if we could see our dreams and study them?

366
00:18:01,410 --> 00:18:02,960
我们能够一种比之前
Could we know each other

367
00:18:03,080 --> 00:18:07,350
更具有意义的方式来了解对方么？
in a more profound way than ever before?

368
00:18:08,350 --> 00:18:10,910
在平均一个人一生中，
During an average life span,

369
00:18:11,020 --> 00:18:14,070
一个人大约花6年的时间做梦。
a human being spends about six years dreaming.

370
00:18:14,190 --> 00:18:17,510
这比我们大脑无意识的臆想
That's more than 52,000 hours of imagery

371
00:18:17,620 --> 00:18:20,760
还多了52,000个小时。
buzzing through our unconscious brains.

372
00:18:25,700 --> 00:18:28,660
计算神经科学家神谷有纪
Computational neuroscientist Yuki Kamitani

373
00:18:28,770 --> 00:18:31,360
认为有一天观看和记录
believes one day it will be possible

374
00:18:31,470 --> 00:18:34,910
人们的梦境是可能的。
to watch and record what people are dreaming.

375
00:18:36,640 --> 00:18:37,970
如果可行的话，
When that happens,

376
00:18:38,080 --> 00:18:40,730
我们将会在一个更深层次的水平上
we will all get to know ourselves

377
00:18:40,850 --> 00:18:42,140
了解我们自己。
on a much deeper level.

378
00:18:42,250 --> 00:18:45,570
我认为如果我们重建
I believe that if we can reconstruct

379
00:18:45,690 --> 00:18:48,080
或者解码一个梦境的内容，
or decode the contents of a dream,

380
00:18:48,190 --> 00:18:50,740
我们就可以揭示我们的身份。
the identity is revealed.

381
00:18:50,860 --> 00:18:54,380
如果我们记得我们的梦境，
If we remember our dreams,

382
00:18:54,490 --> 00:18:58,300
那通常会是一系列充斥情绪的图像。
it is often as a series of emotionally charged images.

383
00:18:59,970 --> 00:19:02,560
实际上，科学家已经发现
In fact, scientists have found

384
00:19:02,670 --> 00:19:05,760
当人们在做梦的时候视觉皮质
that the visual cortex of a dreaming brain

385
00:19:05,870 --> 00:19:07,130
非常活跃。
is highly active.

386
00:19:07,240 --> 00:19:10,530
其中的电信号的模式
Patterns of electrical activity wash over it,

387
00:19:10,640 --> 00:19:12,370
使有纪思考
which makes Yuki wonder,

388
00:19:12,480 --> 00:19:14,940
我们能否阅读这些模式
can we learn to read those patterns

389
00:19:15,050 --> 00:19:18,010
并将其转换成电脑上可以看到的图像？
and convert them into images on a computer?

390
00:19:18,120 --> 00:19:23,540
大脑活动可以看做是一个密码或一条加密的信息
Brain activity can be seen as a code or an encrypted message

391
00:19:23,660 --> 00:19:27,080
这个信息就是关于这个视觉世界中发生的事情。
about what's going on in the visual world.

392
00:19:27,190 --> 00:19:31,120
我们大脑中图像的模式
The patterns of images we make in our brains

393
00:19:31,230 --> 00:19:32,490
是高度扭曲的，
are highly distorted,

394
00:19:32,600 --> 00:19:35,220
就像一副破碎的眼镜
in the same way a pair of shattered glasses

395
00:19:35,340 --> 00:19:37,500
扭曲了我们的视野。
distorts our view of the world.

396
00:19:39,040 --> 00:19:40,390
但是如果我们搜集
But if we collected data

397
00:19:40,510 --> 00:19:43,760
通过这些破碎镜片看到的上百幅图像的数据，
on hundreds of images seen through those shattered lenses,

398
00:19:43,880 --> 00:19:45,470
我们可以在这些扭曲的图像中
we could find a correspondence

399
00:19:45,580 --> 00:19:48,570
找到与真实相一致的图像。
between the distorted images and the real ones.

400
00:19:48,680 --> 00:19:50,540
这可能需要一些时间，
It might take a while,

401
00:19:50,650 --> 00:19:54,480
但是如果我们使用一台强大的计算机
but if we gave that job to a powerful computer,

402
00:19:54,590 --> 00:19:58,810
它或许能偶将一些混乱的图像解码成可以识别的图像。
it could decode the scrambled images into recognizable ones.

403
00:19:58,930 --> 00:20:01,650
这就是Yuki如何试图破解密码
And this is how Yuki tries to crack the code

404
00:20:01,760 --> 00:20:02,850
将这些图片
that turns images

405
00:20:02,960 --> 00:20:06,920
转换成视觉皮层的活动模式。
into patterns of activity in the visual cortex.

406
00:20:07,030 --> 00:20:10,320
我们测量人类受试者的大脑活动，
We measure the brain activity of human subjects,

407
00:20:10,440 --> 00:20:13,390
我们让受试者进入扫描仪
and we let the subject go into the scanner

408
00:20:13,510 --> 00:20:15,430
接受脑部扫描。
and scan their brain.

409
00:20:15,540 --> 00:20:16,900
在这个过程中，
And during that,

410
00:20:17,010 --> 00:20:19,500
我们给受试者呈现出了几幅图。
we present some images to the subject.

411
00:20:19,610 --> 00:20:22,700
一般一个单独的实验中
Typically several hundreds or thousands of images

412
00:20:22,820 --> 00:20:24,140
会有几百甚至上千张图片。
in single experiment.

413
00:20:24,250 --> 00:20:26,470
Yuki给人们看的这些图片
The images Yuki shows people

414
00:20:26,590 --> 00:20:28,240
只是一些简单的黑白图形——
are simple black-and-white shapes --

415
00:20:28,360 --> 00:20:31,910
一个方块，一个十字，一条线。
a square, a cross, a line.

416
00:20:32,030 --> 00:20:34,350
通过使用一个强大的计算机阵列，
Using a powerful computer array,

417
00:20:34,460 --> 00:20:37,850
他记录下了视觉皮层活动的
he records the precise pattern of activity

418
00:20:37,960 --> 00:20:39,650
精确模式。
in the visual cortex.

419
00:20:39,770 --> 00:20:42,420
在对同一个人几次实验之后，
After multiple trials with the same person,

420
00:20:42,540 --> 00:20:44,460
计算机懂得如何区分
the computer learns to distinguish

421
00:20:44,570 --> 00:20:46,630
每幅图所引发的活动模式。
the patterns triggered by each image.

422
00:20:46,740 --> 00:20:47,960
换句话说，
In other words,

423
00:20:48,080 --> 00:20:51,370
计算机可以通过大脑活动
the computer can judge purely from the brain activity

424
00:20:51,480 --> 00:20:54,640
纯粹的判断出受试者正在看的什么图片。
which of the shapes the subject is looking at.

425
00:20:54,750 --> 00:20:57,300
接下来，Yuki做了一些卓越的工作。
And then Yuki does something remarkable.

426
00:20:57,420 --> 00:21:01,340
他给受试者看一些新图，
He shows the subjects brand-new images,

427
00:21:01,460 --> 00:21:04,010
一些电脑也从未见过的图片，
images the computer has never seen,

428
00:21:04,120 --> 00:21:07,150
这时让电脑描绘出
and lets the computer try to draw a picture

429
00:21:07,260 --> 00:21:10,020
受试者所看到的图像。
of what the subjects are seeing.

430
00:21:10,130 --> 00:21:11,950
这些图片是
These are the images

431
00:21:12,070 --> 00:21:14,720
电脑通过阅读人类大脑得到的。
the computer reads inside people's brains.

432
00:21:14,840 --> 00:21:19,990
这些是受试者看的图片。
And these are the images they are actually looking at.

433
00:21:20,110 --> 00:21:21,560
这是首次
This is the first time

434
00:21:21,680 --> 00:21:25,070
有人可以通过仅观察大脑活动
anyone has been able to know what people are seeing

435
00:21:25,180 --> 00:21:27,430
而知道人们正在看什么。
purely by looking at their brains.

436
00:21:27,550 --> 00:21:30,740
看这个视觉皮层，
Looking at the visual cortex,

437
00:21:30,850 --> 00:21:35,180
我们成功重建出了这些被看到的图片。
we have just succeeded in reconstructing seen images.

438
00:21:35,290 --> 00:21:38,980
我们现在试图重建出想象的图片
We are now trying to reconstruct imagined images

439
00:21:39,090 --> 00:21:42,860
或是你梦境中的图像。
or images in your dreams.

440
00:21:44,660 --> 00:21:46,720
Yuki的这个方法，
Yuki's method, as yet,

441
00:21:46,830 --> 00:21:49,720
还只能应用于简单的黑白图案，
only works on pixilated black-and-white images,

442
00:21:49,840 --> 00:21:52,390
但是经过几年的改进，
but with a few more years of refinement,

443
00:21:52,510 --> 00:21:55,660
Yuki相信我们是可以记录下我们的梦境的
Yuki believes we will be able to record our dreams

444
00:21:55,780 --> 00:21:59,030
这次则是全彩色、高分辨率的电影。
as full-color, high-definition movies.

445
00:21:59,150 --> 00:22:03,040
这将是一扇进入我们灵魂的窗户。
And that would truly be a window into our souls.

446
00:22:03,150 --> 00:22:06,270
那些我们思维中无意识的层面
Those, you know, unconscious aspects of our mind

447
00:22:06,390 --> 00:22:10,040
决定了我们是谁和我们的身份。
defines what we are and what the identity is.

448
00:22:10,160 --> 00:22:14,920
我认为如果我们能够揭示梦境
So, I think if we can reveal some dream contents

449
00:22:15,030 --> 00:22:17,920
那些人们并没有意识到的梦境，
which someone is not aware of,

450
00:22:18,030 --> 00:22:24,940
接下来我们可能能够揭示一个人深层次的性质。
then that might reveal some deep property of that person.

451
00:22:26,710 --> 00:22:28,460
我们的真实身份
Our true identities

452
00:22:28,580 --> 00:22:32,170
很快就能暴露在我们眼前，
could soon be laid bare for all to see,

453
00:22:32,280 --> 00:22:35,170
包括我们不想被人看到的部分，
including the parts we don't want seen,

454
00:22:35,280 --> 00:22:39,640
就像我们思维最深处的秘密和幻想。
like our deepest-held secrets and fantasies.

455
00:22:39,750 --> 00:22:42,940
但是你可以不用担心。
But you may not have to worry.

456
00:22:43,060 --> 00:22:47,010
因为编辑我们思维的力量
Because the power to edit the contents of our minds

457
00:22:47,130 --> 00:22:49,530
就在眼前。
is close at hand.

458
00:22:52,180 --> 00:22:56,200
我们的脑充满着各种记忆。
Our brains are filled with memories.

459
00:22:56,320 --> 00:22:58,540
有些让我们欢乐。
Some of them bring us joy.

460
00:22:58,650 --> 00:23:01,940
而有些是我们希望能够忘记的。
Others make us wish we could forget.

461
00:23:02,060 --> 00:23:04,210
不论我们是否喜欢，
Whether we like it or not,

462
00:23:04,320 --> 00:23:08,350
我们的记忆决定了我们的思维和行为。
our memories shape how we think and how we act.

463
00:23:08,460 --> 00:23:14,090
但是现在有一组研究者认为他们已经发现了一种方法
But now one group of researchers thinks it has found a way

464
00:23:14,200 --> 00:23:20,510
可以用来改变记忆，或许改变我们是谁。
to change memory and perhaps change who we are.

465
00:23:23,910 --> 00:23:27,440
我们能专门改变我们的身份感么？
Can we deliberately change our sense of identity?

466
00:23:27,550 --> 00:23:32,770
来自于纽约州立大学的神经系统学家Andre Fenton
Neuroscientist Andre Fenton from the State University of New York

467
00:23:32,890 --> 00:23:34,680
不明白为什么这样不行。
doesn't see why not.

468
00:23:34,790 --> 00:23:36,480
对于他来说，大脑
To him, the brain

469
00:23:36,590 --> 00:23:39,450
和它内部的神经元之间的联系
and its pathways of connections between neurons

470
00:23:39,560 --> 00:23:42,650
就像纽约城迷宫般的街道——
are like the labyrinth of streets in New York City --

471
00:23:42,760 --> 00:23:46,490
一个他平常跑步于其中的迷宫。
a maze he navigates on his daily runs.

472
00:23:46,600 --> 00:23:48,720
就像曼哈顿的交通，
And just like Manhattan traffic,

473
00:23:48,840 --> 00:23:51,290
电流在大脑中传递时的环境
conditions for the flow of electricity around the brain

474
00:23:51,410 --> 00:23:55,100
在每条路径上都是不一样的。
are not the same on every route.

475
00:23:55,210 --> 00:23:57,000
如果你经历了一些事，
If you experience something,

476
00:23:57,110 --> 00:23:59,570
那里就会存在过电信号激活
there's been an electrical activation

477
00:23:59,680 --> 00:24:01,100
而在脑中
somewhere in the brain

478
00:24:01,220 --> 00:24:03,000
电信号会传播到整个大脑，
that spreads through the brain,

479
00:24:03,120 --> 00:24:04,810
这就形成了你的经历。
and that is your experience.

480
00:24:04,920 --> 00:24:06,240
就像在一座城市中，
As in a city,

481
00:24:06,350 --> 00:24:09,380
有许多道路连接一个街区和另一个街区，
there are roads that connect one district to another district,

482
00:24:09,490 --> 00:24:12,250
这些道路可能会是林荫大道
and those roads can be very big boulevards

483
00:24:12,360 --> 00:24:14,050
车流量会非常大，
that send a lot of traffic,

484
00:24:14,160 --> 00:24:18,320
也可能是一些小巷，传送一些特殊信息，
or they can be small alleys that send very specific information,

485
00:24:18,430 --> 00:24:21,720
但是并不会很快，也不会很容易。
but nonetheless, not very rapidly or very easily.

486
00:24:21,840 --> 00:24:23,620
许多年，
For years,

487
00:24:23,740 --> 00:24:26,130
科学家认为我们脑中的这些途径
scientists thought the pathways in our brains

488
00:24:26,240 --> 00:24:29,230
在我们从婴儿长成成人后就固化了。
were set in stone after we matured from babies to adults.

489
00:24:29,340 --> 00:24:31,870
小巷就再不能变宽。
Alleys could not become wider.

490
00:24:31,980 --> 00:24:34,600
高速公路也不会变窄。
Highways could not become narrower.

491
00:24:34,720 --> 00:24:36,540
但是现在情况变了
But now it has become clear

492
00:24:36,650 --> 00:24:39,110
成人大脑中的道路
that the roads in our adult brains

493
00:24:39,220 --> 00:24:41,280
是处于不断重建中的。
are under constant construction.

494
00:24:41,390 --> 00:24:44,750
每次我们储存一个新记忆，
Every time we store a new memory,

495
00:24:44,860 --> 00:24:48,380
电流就会传向无数个神经元。
electrical activity propagates through millions of neurons.

496
00:24:48,500 --> 00:24:52,050
就像Andre只能去找一条新路
Just as Andre  is forced to find a new route

497
00:24:52,170 --> 00:24:54,090
如果他的路被封锁了，
if his pathway is blocked,

498
00:24:54,200 --> 00:24:57,090
我们的神经通路也会调整他们自己
our neural pathways adjust themselves

499
00:24:57,200 --> 00:25:00,130
来处理并记录新的经历。
to process and record new experiences.

500
00:25:00,240 --> 00:25:03,000
这样，神经系统学家了解了
And so, what neuroscientists understand is

501
00:25:03,110 --> 00:25:07,030
我们一生中有足够多的
that there's a sufficient amount of this plasticity

502
00:25:07,150 --> 00:25:08,440
可塑性，
throughout life,

503
00:25:08,550 --> 00:25:10,340
这受到
and that it is affected

504
00:25:10,450 --> 00:25:13,840
经验的控制调整。
and modulated and controlled by experience.

505
00:25:13,950 --> 00:25:17,010
最近，科学家鉴定出
Recently, scientists have identified

506
00:25:17,120 --> 00:25:18,680
大脑中的一个分子
a molecule in the brain

507
00:25:18,790 --> 00:25:23,080
它在我们形成新记忆时发挥作用。
that jumps into action when we are forming new memories.

508
00:25:23,200 --> 00:25:27,620
它叫做PKMzeta。
It is called PKMzeta.

509
00:25:27,730 --> 00:25:30,890
PKMzeta意思是“蛋白激酶mzeta”。
PKMzeta stands for "protein kinase mzeta."

510
00:25:31,000 --> 00:25:32,470
这是我最喜欢的分子。
It's my favorite molecule.

511
00:25:34,570 --> 00:25:40,100
当PKMzeta收到消息调整神经元时，
When PKMzeta gets told to deploy in a neuron,

512
00:25:40,210 --> 00:25:42,300
它就会基于
it gets told to do that

513
00:25:42,420 --> 00:25:45,440
最近的经历这么做，
on the basis of a recent experience,

514
00:25:45,550 --> 00:25:48,940
它的工作就是调控或增加
and what it does is it mediates efficient

515
00:25:49,060 --> 00:25:53,380
神经传输的效率。
or increased efficiency of neural transmission.

516
00:25:53,490 --> 00:25:56,450
当一个记忆需要通过它在
When a memory needs to navigate its way

517
00:25:56,560 --> 00:25:58,790
我们脑中的记忆时，
through the traffic of our brains,

518
00:25:58,900 --> 00:26:01,320
PKMzeta就可以清空道路，
PKMzeta clears the way,

519
00:26:01,440 --> 00:26:05,460
确保记忆可以安全的进入长期记忆阶段。
making sure the memory safely reaches long-term storage.

520
00:26:05,570 --> 00:26:07,700
这些长期记忆，
Those long-term memories,

521
00:26:07,810 --> 00:26:11,000
这时形成并可以永远保持，
the ones that you form now and you will keep forever,

522
00:26:11,110 --> 00:26:16,900
这类的信息储存似乎受到PKMzeta的调控。
that kind of information storage seems to be mediated by PKMzeta.

523
00:26:17,020 --> 00:26:19,570
但是Andre知道一种化学物质
But Andre knew of a chemical

524
00:26:19,690 --> 00:26:22,110
可以中和PKMzeta，
that could neutralize PKMzeta,

525
00:26:22,220 --> 00:26:26,110
称为zeta抑制肽，或ZIP，
called zeta inhibitory peptide, or ZIP,

526
00:26:26,230 --> 00:26:31,020
他想如果他将其注射进活的大脑中，
and he wondered if he injected it into a living brain,

527
00:26:31,130 --> 00:26:34,320
是否可以阻止形成长期记忆。
could he prevent it from forming long-term memories.

528
00:26:34,430 --> 00:26:37,790
